<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Serbia and Montenegro</h1><p class="st-meta">2003 CE – 2006 CE · capital Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Loose union of Serbia and Montenegro that replaced the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia and ended when Montenegro voted for independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By 2001 Montenegro's government sought independence from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>. To avoid a sudden break, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-solana">Javier Solana</a>, brokered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> Agreement of 2002. A Constitutional Charter adopted on 4 February 2003 turned the federation into the State Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, with Belgrade as its seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The union had a single-chamber parliament, a president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svetozar-marovic">Svetozar Marović</a>, and a small council of ministers handling foreign affairs, defence and human rights. Otherwise the two republics ran their own affairs: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> used the euro and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> the dinar, with separate customs systems. The charter allowed either member to hold an independence referendum after three years.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In a referendum on 21 May 2006, 55.5 percent of Montenegrin voters chose independence, just above the 55 percent threshold set by the EU. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> declared independence on 3 June 2006, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> became the union's legal successor.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/serbia-and-montenegro.jpg" alt="Serbia and Montenegro" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. (www.gov.yu) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro_(1992%E2%80%932006).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2003 CE – 2006 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belgrade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Svetozar Marović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>State Union of Serbia and Montenegro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2004&amp;focus=serbia-and-montenegro" aria-label="Serbia and Montenegro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Serbia and Montenegro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5488 850M5488 850L5488 850M5488 850L5467 924"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0M5488 850h0M5467 924h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Belgrade. <a href="/atlas?year=2004&amp;focus=serbia-and-montenegro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
